The tar command in Linux is used to archive multiple files and directories into a single file. It doesn’t compress by itself, but it’s often combined with compression tools like gzip or bzip2. The resulting file is called a tarball (usually with .tar, .tar.gz, or .tar.bz2 extensions).
Syntax:
tar [options] archive_name.tar file1 file2 ..
------Common Options
Option	Meaning
-c	Create a new archive
-x	Extract files from an archive
-v	Verbose (show progress)
-f	Specify archive file name
-z	Use gzip compression
-j	Use bzip2 compression

-----Create an archive
tar -cvf backup.tar file1.txt file2.txt
-c → create
-v → show progress
-f backup.tar → name of the archive
This makes backup.tar containing file1.txt and file2.txt.....

-----Create a compressed archive (gzip)
tar -czvf backup.tar.gz myfolder/
-z → compress with gzip
This creates a compressed archive of myfolder....

-----Extract an archive
tar -xvf backup.tar
Extracts all files from backup.tar into the current directory.

------Extract a compressed archive
tar -xzvf backup.tar.gz
Extracts files from a gzip-compressed tarball.

------Extract to a specific directory
tar -xvf backup.tar -C /home/teja/extracted/
Extracts files into /home/teja/extracted/
